{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe overestimation bias: The model consistently forecasts a smooth, monotonic trend (either rising or falling) while the true market exhibits high volatility with sharp spikes followed by rapid mean reversion. Apply a volatility dampener to flatten the forecast curve.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after spikes: When the true price spikes (e.g., from ~84 to ~90), the model fails to anticipate the subsequent crash. If sentiment is positive but price action shows a sudden peak, immediately adjust the forecast downward to reflect the historical pattern of a 10-15% correction within 5-7 days.",
    "Reduce forecast horizon lag: The model reacts too slowly to trend reversals. If the last 3 days of history show a sharp drop (e.g., 88 to 76), the model's 30-day forecast should not remain elevated; force a steeper downward slope in the first 10 days of the forecast window.",
    "Decouple sentiment from price trajectory: High positive sentiment scores (1.00) often precede price peaks followed by crashes, yet the model interprets this as a signal for sustained growth. Invert the sentiment signal for the short-term forecast (next 5-10 days) when sentiment hits 1.00 after a rapid price increase, anticipating a reversal rather than continuation.",
    "Adjust for cyclical floor/ceiling behavior: The model forecasts prices exceeding 90 or dropping below 76 without oscillation. Constrain forecasts to oscillate around the 18-day moving average rather than drifting linearly, as the market tends to revert to the mean after hitting local extremes."
  ]
}
